/*!
    \page qtros2-sensor-message-types.html
    \title Choosing Sensor Message Types
    \ingroup qtros2-guides

    \keyword lidar
    \keyword LiDAR
    \keyword LIDAR
    \keyword laser scanner
    \keyword point cloud
    \keyword depth camera
    \keyword IMU
    \keyword camera
    \keyword inertial measurement unit
    \keyword battery
    \keyword battery level
    \keyword state of charge
    \keyword SLAM
    \keyword simultaneous localization and mapping
    \keyword occupancy grid
    \keyword odometry
    \keyword joint state
    \keyword audio
    \keyword microphone
    \keyword speech recognition

    \brief A guide to selecting the right ROS 2 sensor message type for common robot hardware.

    Qt ROS2 provides QML-friendly wrappers for the standard \c sensor_msgs and \c nav_msgs
    message types.  This page helps you pick the right message type for your hardware and
    explains which messages SLAM and navigation stacks expect or produce.

    \note All message types listed here are from \c sensor_msgs, \c nav_msgs, or
    \c geometry_msgs unless stated otherwise.  Audio is a known gap — see
    \l{Audio Sensors and Speech}.

    \section1 Distance and Ranging Sensors

    \table
    \header
        \li Sensor Type
        \li Message Type
        \li Notes
    \row
        \li 2D lidar / laser range-finder
        \li \l laserScan (LaserScan)
        \li Single planar sweep; provides ranges and optional intensities.
    \row
        \li Multi-echo 2D lidar
        \li \l multiEchoLaserScan (MultiEchoLaserScan)
        \li Like LaserScan but each range bin can report multiple return distances.
            Useful for glass, vegetation, or fog detection.
    \row
        \li 3D lidar / depth camera (unordered or 2D-organized)
        \li \l pointCloud2 (PointCloud2)
        \li Flexible binary blob; supports arbitrary per-point fields (x, y, z, intensity, …).
    \row
        \li Ultrasonic / infrared range sensor
        \li \l range (Range)
        \li Single-beam measurement with min/max range bounds and radiation type field
            (ultrasound, infrared).
    \endtable

    \section1 Camera Sensors

    \table
    \header
        \li Sensor Type
        \li Message Type
        \li Notes
    \row
        \li RGB or mono camera
        \li \l image (Image)
        \li Uncompressed; the Qt wrapper exposes a \c QImage property for direct use in QML.
            Encoding field selects colour format (rgb8, rgba8, bgr8, mono8, mono16, …).
    \row
        \li Compressed camera stream (JPEG / PNG)
        \li \l compressedImage (CompressedImage)
        \li Smaller over-the-wire size; the Qt wrapper decodes to a \c QImage on demand.
    \row
        \li Camera calibration parameters
        \li \l cameraInfo (CameraInfo)
        \li Accompanies Image messages; carries intrinsics, distortion, and projection matrices.
            Subscribe alongside Image on a synchronised pair of topics.
    \row
        \li Region of interest within a camera image
        \li \l regionOfInterest (RegionOfInterest)
        \li Embedded in CameraInfo; also used standalone to flag a crop window.
    \endtable

    \section1 Inertial and Navigation Sensors

    \table
    \header
        \li Sensor Type
        \li Message Type
        \li Notes
    \row
        \li IMU (accelerometer + gyroscope ± magnetometer)
        \li \l imu (Imu)
        \li Reports orientation, angular velocity, and linear acceleration each with a 3×3
            covariance matrix.  Set covariance diagonal to -1 if a field is not measured.
    \row
        \li GPS / GNSS receiver
        \li \l navSatFix (NavSatFix)
        \li Latitude, longitude, altitude and position covariance; \c status field indicates
            fix quality (no fix, fix, SBAS fix, GBAS fix).
    \row
        \li Magnetic compass
        \li \l magneticField (MagneticField)
        \li 3-axis field vector in Tesla with covariance.
    \row
        \li External time reference (GPS PPS, NTP, PTP)
        \li \l timeReference (TimeReference)
        \li Stamps a measurement from an external clock so the ROS graph can correlate it with
            system time.  Useful for hardware-synchronised camera rigs and lidar sweeps.
    \endtable

    \section1 Power and Battery

    \table
    \header
        \li Sensor Type
        \li Message Type
        \li Notes
    \row
        \li Battery or power supply
        \li \l batteryState (BatteryState)
        \li Covers voltage, current, temperature, charge, capacity, and state-of-charge
            percentage (0.0–1.0).  Enum constants identify chemistry (LiPo, NiMH, …),
            health (good, overheat, dead, …), and charging status (charging, discharging,
            full, …).  Multi-cell packs can report per-cell voltage and temperature arrays.
    \endtable

    \section1 Environmental Sensors

    \table
    \header
        \li Sensor Type
        \li Message Type
        \li Notes
    \row
        \li Barometer / pressure sensor
        \li \l fluidPressure (FluidPressure)
        \li Absolute pressure in Pascals with variance.
    \row
        \li Temperature sensor
        \li \l temperature (Temperature)
        \li Temperature in degrees Celsius with variance.
    \row
        \li Humidity sensor
        \li \l relativeHumidity (RelativeHumidity)
        \li Relative humidity as a ratio (0.0–1.0) with variance.
    \row
        \li Illuminance / ambient light
        \li \l illuminance (Illuminance)
        \li Illuminance in lux with variance.
    \endtable

    \section1 Actuators and Kinematics

    These messages describe the state of joints and limbs rather than sensors in the
    traditional sense, but they flow through the same sensor pipeline.

    \table
    \header
        \li Use Case
        \li Message Type
        \li Notes
    \row
        \li Robot arm or wheeled-robot joint positions
        \li \l jointState (JointState)
        \li Reports position (rad or m), velocity, and effort per named joint.  Any of the
            three arrays may be left empty if not measured.  Subscribe to \c /joint_states
            to drive a 3D model or display telemetry.
    \row
        \li Floating-base or multi-DOF joints
        \li \l multiDOFJointState (MultiDOFJointState)
        \li Generalises JointState to joints with more than one degree of freedom; each
            joint carries a full Transform, Twist, and Wrench.
    \endtable

    \section1 SLAM and Navigation

    SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping) algorithms consume sensor data and publish
    map and pose estimates.  A Qt dashboard typically subscribes to SLAM outputs to display
    a live map and robot position.

    \section2 SLAM Inputs

    Feed at least one ranging source and optionally an IMU into a SLAM node:

    \table
    \header
        \li Input
        \li Message Type
    \row
        \li 2D lidar sweep
        \li \l laserScan (LaserScan) on \c /scan
    \row
        \li 3D lidar or depth cloud
        \li \l pointCloud2 (PointCloud2) on \c /points
    \row
        \li Wheel odometry (pre-SLAM estimate)
        \li \l odometry (Odometry) on \c /odom
    \row
        \li Inertial measurement
        \li \l imu (Imu) on \c /imu
    \endtable

    \section2 SLAM Outputs

    \table
    \header
        \li Output
        \li Message Type
        \li Notes
    \row
        \li Occupancy grid map
        \li \l occupancyGrid (OccupancyGrid) on \c /map
        \li 2D grid where each cell is free (0), occupied (100), or unknown (-1).
            Subscribe to render a live floor plan.
    \row
        \li Corrected robot pose
        \li \l poseWithCovarianceStamped (PoseWithCovarianceStamped)
        \li SLAM-corrected pose with uncertainty ellipse; broadcast on \c /pose.
    \row
        \li Odometry estimate
        \li \l odometry (Odometry) on \c /odom
        \li Combines pose and twist with covariances; used by the navigation stack.
    \row
        \li Planned path
        \li \l path (Path) on \c /plan
        \li Sequence of poses from the current position to the navigation goal.
    \endtable

    \note SLAM and navigation stacks also broadcast coordinate-frame transforms on the
    \c /tf and \c /tf_static topics using the \c tf2_msgs/TFMessage type.  Qt ROS2 does
    not currently wrap \c tf2_msgs, so transforms must be read through a bridging node or
    a dedicated C++ subscriber if your application needs them directly.

    \section1 Audio Sensors and Speech

    Standard ROS 2 (and the Qt ROS2 Bridge) does not include a message type for raw audio
    data.  The community package \c audio_common_msgs (from the \c audio_common stack)
    provides \c AudioData and \c AudioStamped messages and is widely used, but it is not
    part of the core ROS 2 distribution and is not currently wrapped by Qt ROS2.

    Typical approaches for audio in a ROS 2 system:

    \table
    \header
        \li Task
        \li Common approach
    \row
        \li Capture microphone audio
        \li A dedicated ROS node streams \c audio_common_msgs/AudioData; outside ROS,
            use Qt Multimedia (\c QAudioSource) and bridge results into ROS via a publisher.
    \row
        \li Speech-to-text transcription
        \li A Whisper- or cloud-ASR-based node publishes the transcript as
            \c std_msgs/String on a topic such as \c /speech_recognition/result.
            Subscribe with \l string (String) in QML.
    \row
        \li Text-to-speech output
        \li Send a \c std_msgs/String command to a TTS node; audio playback happens inside
            that node or via Qt Multimedia on the Qt side.
    \row
        \li LLM response piped to speech
        \li Publish the LLM text output as \c std_msgs/String; a downstream TTS node
            converts it to speech.  The full pipeline (mic → ASR → LLM → TTS → speaker)
            runs as ROS nodes; the Qt UI subscribes to the intermediate text topics for
            display and can publish user text input to inject into the chain.
    \endtable

    \section1 Remote Control and Teleoperation

    Sending velocity or steering commands to a robot is covered separately.
    See \l{Teleoperation and Remote Control} for \l joy (Joy),
    \l twist (Twist), and on-screen joystick patterns.

    \sa {Qt ROS2 QML Types}
*/
